module sysl.sync

// How strongly an atomic operation is ordered against the accesses around it (`06 § The kernel
// tier`).
//
// An ordering is not a property of the value being read or written -- every ordering reads the same
// bits -- it is a promise about what *else* is guaranteed to have happened. That is why it is an
// argument rather than a property of the variable: the same word is read with `Relaxed` in a
// statistics counter and with `Acquire` in the handoff that publishes a structure, and only the
// operation knows which it is.
//
// **These are C11's and LLVM's, named the same way**, so the machine documentation and the standards
// text a reader already has apply to them unchanged. Nothing weaker than the machine's own model is
// described here, and nothing here is sysl's invention.
//
// The compiler requires the ordering to be written as one of these names at the call, because it
// becomes a keyword in the instruction rather than a value the instruction reads. See `Atomics`.
enum Ordering

    // No ordering at all: the operation is indivisible and nothing else follows from it. Correct for
    // a counter whose value is read once at the end, and for the increment side of a refcount -- a
    // share you already hold cannot reach zero underneath you (`06 § Refcount ordering`).
    Relaxed

    // Everything the releasing thread wrote before its release is visible here afterwards. Pairs
    // with `Release` on the other side; meaningless on a store, which has nothing to acquire.
    Acquire

    // Everything written before this becomes visible to whoever acquires it. Pairs with `Acquire`;
    // meaningless on a load.
    Release

    // Both, for an operation that is a handoff in each direction at once -- the read-modify-write in
    // the middle of a lock transfer.
    AcqRel

    // Sequentially consistent: every thread agrees on one order for all `SeqCst` operations in the
    // program. The strongest and the slowest, and the default the library surface takes, because it
    // is the one that makes an ordinary program behave the way its author read it.
    SeqCst
